# Commits that changed how the code is laid out and nothing about what it does.
# `git blame` skips them, so the line it names is the one that last changed the
# code rather than the one that last rewrapped it.
#
# This matters more here than in most repos: the convention is that a commit
# message explains the reasoning, so `git blame` is how that reasoning is found.
# A formatting sweep across 62 files points every one of those lines at a commit
# whose message is "reflowed the files", which loses the trail.
#
# Enable it locally - GitHub reads this file automatically, git does not:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
